---
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Docs from "@/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { docHref } from "@/lib/docs";

const install = `pnpm add @statewalker/httpeers-core @statewalker/httpeers-bridge`;

const firstMesh = `import { createRemoteOverLink, serveFetchOverLink } from "@statewalker/httpeers-bridge";
import { pairedLinks } from "@statewalker/httpeers-bridge/ports";
import { createMounts, createPeerRouter, lookupPeer } from "@statewalker/httpeers-core";

// What Bob serves: a mount table of FetchHandlers, (Request) => Promise<Response>.
const mounts = createMounts();
mounts.provide("/hello", async (req) => new Response(\`hello, \${String(lookupPeer(req))}\`));

const [linkA, linkB] = pairedLinks(alice, bob);   // two peer ids, joined over MessageChannel
const stop = await serveFetchOverLink({
  link: linkB,
  dispatch: createPeerRouter({ selfPeerId: bob, mounts, remote: async () => new Response(null, { status: 403 }) }),
});

const call = createRemoteOverLink({ link: linkA });
const res = await call(bob, new Request("http://peer.local/hello"));
await res.text(); // "hello, <alice's peer id>"
await stop();`;

const nodePeer = `import { createMounts } from "@statewalker/httpeers-core";
import { createNode, generateKey, servePeer } from "@statewalker/httpeers-libp2p";
import { nodeTransports } from "@statewalker/httpeers-libp2p/node";

const node = await createNode({
  privateKey: await generateKey(),
  transports: nodeTransports(),
  listen: ["/ip4/0.0.0.0/tcp/0"],
});
const mounts = createMounts();
const peer = await servePeer({ node, mounts, access: guard }); // guard: e.g. withAccess(...)

const res = await peer.call(otherPeerId, new Request("http://peer.local/hello"));`;

const member = `import { startMember } from "@statewalker/httpeers-member";
import { nodePlatform } from "@statewalker/httpeers-member/node";

const member = await startMember({
  key: "peers",
  mounts,                        // what this peer serves
  rules,                         // the mesh's RuleSet
  config: { relayAddrs, hubPeerId },
  platform: nodePlatform,
  invitationId,                  // omit to resume an existing membership
});

await member.fetch(new Request(\`http://local/peers/\${otherPeer}/hello\`));
member.hubLink();                // "direct" | "relay"`;

const page = `import { createSession } from "@statewalker/httpeers-member/browser";

const session = createSession({
  key: "peers",
  mounts,
  rules,
  onChange: (state) => render(state),
});
await session.start();           // resumes, or waits for an invitation
await session.join(pastedText);  // a join blob, a bare invitation id, or a join URL

// From then on, ordinary page code reaches any member through the ServiceWorker edge:
const res = await fetch(\`/peers/\${peerId}/hello\`);`;

const fromSource = `corepack enable
pnpm install
pnpm build        # every package and app
pnpm test         # real ports, real libp2p nodes`;
---

<Docs
  title="Getting started"
  description="Install httpeers, serve a FetchHandler to another peer, and join a mesh from Node or a browser page."
>
  <h1>Getting started</h1>
  <p>
    httpeers is a set of small libraries with one contract running through all of them:
    <code>FetchHandler = (Request) =&gt; Promise&lt;Response&gt;</code>. A mount table routes it, a peer serves
    it over libp2p, and an edge lets a page reach it through its own <code>fetch()</code>. You can start at
    the bottom, with two peers in one process, and add the transport, the hub and the browser edge as you
    need them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before building on it, read the <a href="/docs/security">security model</a>: the mesh mounts every peer's
    HTTP surface into your origin and calls it with your identity automatically, so <em>data</em> crosses that
    boundary safely but <em>code</em> does not.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Two peers, no network</h2>
  <p>
    <code>@statewalker/httpeers-bridge</code> carries HTTP over any duplex. Its <code>./ports</code> entry joins
    two peers back to back over <code>MessageChannel</code>, which is enough to write and test mesh logic with
    no relay, hub or WebRTC. It runs in Node, workers and browsers.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={install} title="terminal" />
  <CodeBlock code={firstMesh} lang="ts" title="first-mesh.ts" class="mt-4" />
  <p>
    Run it now: the <a href="/examples/mesh-over-ports">two-peers example</a> does exactly this in your tab.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Put a peer on the wire</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={docHref("packages/httpeers-libp2p/README.md")}>httpeers-libp2p</a> is the only package that knows
    libp2p. <code>servePeer</code> puts the same mount table on a libp2p node, and the caller's identity comes from
    the Noise handshake instead of an assertion.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={nodePeer} lang="ts" title="node-peer.ts" />

  <h2>3. Join a mesh</h2>
  <p>
    A mesh has a <strong>hub</strong> that mints invitations and membership tokens
    (<a href={docHref("packages/httpeers-hub/README.md")}>httpeers-hub</a>), and <strong>members</strong> that
    redeem an invitation, keep their link to the hub, advertise, discover and call each other
    (<a href={docHref("packages/httpeers-member/README.md")}>httpeers-member</a>).
    <a href="/docs/membership">Joining a mesh</a> explains the invitation and the heartbeat. A Node member:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={member} lang="ts" title="member.ts" />
  <p>
    In a page, <code>createSession</code> runs the same lifecycle and also mounts a ServiceWorker edge, so ordinary
    page code calls other members with a bare <code>fetch()</code>. The join widget every demo page shares is
    <a href={docHref("packages/httpeers-join/README.md")}>httpeers-join</a>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={page} lang="ts" title="page.ts" />
  <p>
    Who may call what is decided by each target peer, with Biscuit tokens and a Datalog policy
    (<a href={docHref("packages/httpeers-access/README.md")}>httpeers-access</a>), explained in
    <a href="/docs/access">access control</a>; see the <a href="/examples/access-tokens">tokens example</a> and
    the <a href="/examples/hub-invitation">invitation example</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>4. The services a mesh needs</h2>
  <p>
    Browser peers cannot accept inbound connections, so they reserve a slot on a circuit relay. httpeers runs one at
    <code>relay.httpeers.net</code>, with a static-site host for <code>*.httpeers.net</code> and the session shell
    for <code>*.p.httpeers.net</code>. Each is a container image built from <code>apps/</code>; the
    <a href={docHref("deploy/README.md")}>deployment runbook</a> describes the production host, and the
    <a href={docHref("apps/relay/README.md")}>relay's README</a> how to run one on your machine.
  </p>

  <h2>From source</h2>
  <p>Node 24 and pnpm 10 through corepack:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={fromSource} title="terminal" />
  <p>
    Next: <a href="/docs/architecture">the architecture</a>, or the
    <a href="/examples">live examples</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
